{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-351648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(1)=4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $3\\sqrt{6}$ | $\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f129727f06399c32b1c5555ffe5852562ff50df36beb95790acb546bab56b497"} {"question" "q-351648" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351648" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-120841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{e}^{-y},\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-120841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{e}^{-y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{e}} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{e}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4\\sqrt{e}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{e}}$ | $\\frac{3}{4\\sqrt{e}}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-120841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f129727f06399c32b1c5555ffe5852562ff50df36beb95790acb546bab56b497"} {"question" "q-120841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-120841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\sqrt{e}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4\\sqrt{e}}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{e}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{e}}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-120841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4\\sqrt{e}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-348069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(4)=4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-348069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =4+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}$ | $\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-348069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f129727f06399c32b1c5555ffe5852562ff50df36beb95790acb546bab56b497"} {"question" "q-348069" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-348069" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3\\sqrt{xy},\\,y(4)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =4+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3\\sqrt{xy} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $4$ | $9\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f129727f06399c32b1c5555ffe5852562ff50df36beb95790acb546bab56b497"} {"question" "q-351669" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351669" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2y),\\,y(1)=\\frac{e}{2}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{e}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{e}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{e+1}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1) \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{e}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{e+1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)$ | $\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+1)}{4}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f129727f06399c32b1c5555ffe5852562ff50df36beb95790acb546bab56b497"} {"question" "q-351652" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351652" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}\\ln(e+1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-53858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-53858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-53858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f129727f06399c32b1c5555ffe5852562ff50df36beb95790acb546bab56b497"} {"question" "q-53858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-53858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-53858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+2,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y\\,(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2}).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is $\\frac{3}{2},$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2}-0)}{2}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+2=2\\cdot 0+2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =1+Δy=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+2=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{21}{8} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{21}{8}=\\frac{41}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{8}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{41}{8}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y\\,(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{41}{8}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f129727f06399c32b1c5555ffe5852562ff50df36beb95790acb546bab56b497"} {"question" "q-351664" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-351664" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{41}{8}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-117949" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-117949" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}={1}^{2}-{2}^{2}=-3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-3)\\cdot 1=-3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2-3=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-3$ |\n| $2$ | $-1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}={2}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+3=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-3$ |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7e