65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-348078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(6).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=6$ is $6,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-0}{3}=\\frac{6}{3}=2\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={0}^{2}+3(1)=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 2=6 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+6=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={2}^{2}+3(7)=25 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=25\\cdot 2=50 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+2=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=7+50=57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ | $25$ | $50$ |\n| $4$ | $57$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+3y={4}^{2}+3(57)=187 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=187\\cdot 2=374 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=4+2=6 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=57+374=431\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ | $25$ | $50$ |\n| $4$ | $57$ | $187$ | $374$ |\n| $6$ | $431$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(6)≈431.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-348078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f129727f06399c32b1c5555ffe5852562ff50df36beb95790acb546bab56b497"} {"question" "q-348078" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "q-348078" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "431"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "27d678aedff0de7e07b9679d561b056d6b5a65cbde78ed7edb38edce76f367ee"} {"question" "e-7701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'=xy^4, \\qquad y(2) = 1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/example-7701.json" "file_sha256" "397e672e68fb91e2fddf39cda03f507c95d3dbfe23cd2549fe5c659ce7f28f09"} {"question" "e-7701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$\\Delta{y}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (2)^{4} \\\\  & =40 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.  \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ | $40$ | $20$ |\n\nTherefore, the missing value is $\\Delta y = 20.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/example-7701.json" "file_sha256" "397e672e68fb91e2fddf39cda03f507c95d3dbfe23cd2549fe5c659ce7f28f09"} {"question" "e-537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=3,\n$$\n\nuse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(2)$ using two steps of equal size." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/example-537.json" "file_sha256" "9f0afaa119ed23c739b16b3244fcdd961ade1a88f488b7328a9d704bebbeab1b"} {"question" "e-537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=3.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+3=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y' \\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 6=18 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=9 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=6+9=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $18$ | $9$ |\n| $2$ | $15$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(2) \\approx 15.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/example-537.json" "file_sha256" "9f0afaa119ed23c739b16b3244fcdd961ade1a88f488b7328a9d704bebbeab1b"} {"question" "e-9354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^3-y^2, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/example-9354.json" "file_sha256" "a78c187e405e594bf97414a03f18f859cf5a3865d8a743af18e296cbe3a67d73"} {"question" "e-9354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-0}{3}=\\dfrac{3}{3} = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={0}^{3}-{1}^{2}=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={1}^{3}-{0}^{2}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={2}^{3}-{1}^{2}=7 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=7\\cdot 1=7 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+7=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $3$ | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)\\approx 8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/example-9354.json" "file_sha256" "a78c187e405e594bf97414a03f18f859cf5a3865d8a743af18e296cbe3a67d73"}] "usage" {"e-537" [] "e-7701" [] "e-9354" []} "basis" 1285 "retractions" [[17592186175729 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y'=xy^4, \\qquad y(2) = 1$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{2}$?\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | $\\,? \\,$ |"] [17592186175729 :question/worked-solution "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx \\\\\n&= 2 + \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2} \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\n&= 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute\n$\\Delta{y}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= xy^{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2} \\cdot (2)^{4} \\\\\n&= 40 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 40 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ | $40$ | $20$ |\n\nTherefore, the missing value is\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = 20\n$$"] [17592186175700 :question/problem "Given the initial value problem\n$y' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=3$,\nuse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(2)$ using two steps of equal size."] [17592186175700 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2)$. The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1$, and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=3$.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2xy = 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 3 = 6 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = 3 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 3 + 3 = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y' \\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2xy = 2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 6 = 18 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 18 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = 9 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = 2 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 6 + 9 = 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $18$ | $9$ |\n| $2$ | $15$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx 15\n$$"] [17592186175794 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y' = x^3-y^2, \\qquad y(0)=1$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3)$."] [17592186175794 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3)$. The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3$, and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-0}{3}=\\dfrac{3}{3} = 1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1$.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{3} - y^{2} = 0^{3} - 1^{2} =-1 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx =-1 \\cdot 1 =-1 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 0 + 1 = 1 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 1 + (-1) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{3} - y^{2} = 1^{3} - 0^{2} = 1 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 1 \\cdot 1 = 1 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + 1 = 2 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 0 + 1 = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{3} - y^{2} = 2^{3} - 1^{2} = 7 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 7 \\cdot 1 = 7 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 2 + 1 = 3 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 1 + 7 = 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $3$ | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(3)\\approx 8\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
